  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several common ways soft or flexible materials can transform into 3D shapes when different layers or structures respond differently to mechanical or environmental cues. The basic idea is that combining a stimuli-responsive material with a non-responsive one creates internal strain mismatch, which drives deformation.\n\nAcross the figure, this mismatch produces four main shape changes: rolling, folding, curving, and buckling. In rolling, a bilayer bends continuously into an arc or tube-like form, illustrated with a layered strip and a cable-like device. In folding, patterned responsive regions create sharper hinges, so a flat strip bends at specific locations into angular shapes. In curving, a conductive layer on a soft substrate bends smoothly, suggesting flexible electronics that can conform to curved surfaces. In buckling, a thin 2D precursor attached to a pre-stretched substrate rises into a 3D form when the substrate is released, creating wave-like or dome-like architectures.\n\nOverall, the figure compares how layer arrangement, patterned active sites, and substrate pre-strain can be used to convert initially flat structures into controlled three-dimensional geometries for devices and engineered materials.",
